{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST locate its per-user configuration at\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/habeas/config.toml` on Unix when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is an absolute,\nnonempty path, otherwise at `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`. On Windows it MUST\nuse `%APPDATA%/habeas/config.toml`. `HOME` and `APPDATA` MUST likewise be\nabsolute, nonempty paths to be used. If no usable base directory can be\ndetermined, an operation requiring user configuration MUST fail with an\nexplanatory diagnostic naming the variable.\n\nThe same user configuration MUST be usable across projects. Its location MUST\nNOT be selected relative to the current project or changed by project settings.\nUser-local preferences for a project MAY remain in the user configuration;\nworkspace settings MUST use `habeas.toml` under\n{cite}`requirements.configuration.workspace_location`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.configuration.user_location`. The implementation matches the requirement, and the tests cover its interesting cases.\n\n**Implementation** (`src/config.rs`, all marked `{impl}`):\n- `user_config_path` calls `user_config_path_from(ConfigPlatform::current(), env)`. It takes no project argument, so the location cannot depend on the current project or on project settings.\n- On Unix, `user_config_path_from` uses `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` when `absolute_directory` accepts it. `absolute_directory` accepts a value only if `PathBuf::is_absolute()`, which rejects empty and relative values. Otherwise it falls back to `required_directory(\"HOME\")` joined with `.config`. The result is `<base>/habeas/config.toml`.\n- On Windows it requires an absolute `APPDATA` and appends `habeas/config.toml`.\n- `HOME` and `APPDATA` go through `required_directory`. When either is missing, empty or relative, it returns an `io::Error`. Its message is \"cannot determine Habeas directory: {name} must be an absolute, nonempty path\", which names the variable. `resolve_authoring` calls `user_config_path()?`, so an operation that needs user configuration fails with that diagnostic.\n- `resolve_authoring` is the only place in `src` that calls `user_config_path`. No unmarked code computes the user config path.\n- User-local project preferences stay in the user config (`authoring.projects`). Workspace settings load from `habeas.toml` via the separate `workspace_location` implementation (`workspace_document`). That requirement is only cited here and is carried out by its own text.\n- I found no behavior in the marked code that the requirement does not specify. The code shared with `state_location` is marked for both requirements.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/configuration.rs`, marked `{test}`):\n- `locations_use_absolute_xdg_values_and_platform_fallbacks` checks:\n  - an absolute `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is used;\n  - empty and relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` values fall back to `$HOME/.config/habeas/config.toml`;\n  - Windows uses `APPDATA`;\n  - nothing set gives an error.\n- `base_directories_must_be_absolute_and_nonempty` checks:\n  - empty and relative `HOME` (Unix) and `APPDATA` (Windows) are rejected, and the error text contains the variable name;\n  - a relative `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` with an absolute `HOME` falls back correctly.\n- Together these cover the main boundaries: absolute, empty, relative and missing values, for both platforms and for the fallback chain. They also cover the error-naming obligation.\n- The project-independence obligation is structural, since the function has no project input, so a dedicated test is not needed.\n\nNo gaps or FIXMEs were found.",
  "references": "a2800054065cedb39cfbad2b80c0a042d1159e4e7bdddddd8ac9e49a6e551787",
